      "title": "Read each absolute value as a distance",
      "body": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> means x is at most 6 from 2. Similarly, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> means y is at most 4 from −2.</p>",
      "recap": "An absolute-value bound describes a two-sided interval.",
      "pitfall": "The centre of the y-interval is −2, not +2.",
      "pause": "Write the two permitted closed intervals.",
